<comment datatype='string'>In the initial study of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-5789&quot;&gt;3-hydroxypropanoate/4-hydroxybutanate cycle&lt;/a&gt; enzymes this enzyme has not been purified from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The two enzymatic activities (later found to be catalyzed by a single enzyme) were measured in cell-free extracts. The crotonyl-CoA hydratase activity, in particular, was extremely high [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18079405&quot;&gt;Berg07&lt;/a&gt;]. 
&lt;p&gt;
In a later study, a search of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; genome revealed a gene (&lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15030&quot;&gt;Msed_0399&lt;/a&gt;) that encodes a fusion protein, composed of an enoyl-CoA hydratase C-terminal domain (~20 kDa) and a dehydrogenase N-terminal domain (~40 kDa). The expected bifunctional hydratase/dehydrogenase activity was confirmed to occur in similar amounts in extracts of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-43687&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Metallosphaera sedula&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, and the protein was purified more than 63-fold to near homogeneity. Mass-spectrometric analysis identified the 70-kDa protein as the product of &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15030&quot;&gt;Msed_0399&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21169482&quot;&gt;RamosVera11&lt;/a&gt;]. 
&lt;p&gt;
The gene was subsequently cloned and expressed in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, and the recombinant protein was purified. Biochemical characterization of the purified recombinant protein confirmed both predicted activities [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21169482&quot;&gt;RamosVera11&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The gene was later cloned (without a His tag) and produced recombinantly in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, along with the &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-10489&quot;&gt;hbs&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-10490&quot;&gt;hbd&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15031&quot;&gt;Msed_0656&lt;/a&gt; genes, which together catalyze the 4 last steps of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-5789&quot;&gt;3-hydroxypropanoate/4-hydroxybutanate cycle&lt;/a&gt;. The predicted combined action of the four enzymes is the conversion of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=4-HYDROXY-BUTYRATE&quot;&gt;4-hydroxybutanoate&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETYL-COA&quot;&gt;acetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;. Indeed, when the four recombinant proteins were combined &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; they were able to catalyze this conversion [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/24532060&quot;&gt;Hawkins14&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
